  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Black-and-gray Leafcutter Bee (Megachile melanophaea) is a solitary bee species belonging to the Megachilidae family, characterized by its distinctive dark coloration and efficient leaf-cutting behavior. This native New England bee is an important pollinator that constructs nests by cutting precise pieces from plant leaves to line its brood cells.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Leaf-cutting behavior",
      "description": "Females cut semicircular pieces from leaves using their large mandibles, a unique and diagnostic behavior visible on host plants",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Black to dark gray body with minimal pale markings, distinguishing it from lighter Megachile species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Abdominal scopa",
      "description": "Dense brush of dark hairs on the underside of the abdomen used for pollen collection",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Black-and-gray Leafcutter Bees prefer open woodlands, gardens, and meadows throughout New England where flowering plants and suitable nesting materials are available.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "These bees are so precise with their leaf-cutting that they can create perfectly shaped circular or oval pieces, which they then roll up and transport to their nests to construct individual brood cells.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
